{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JB6oVk4LZwze5QVLBG5U8a","type":"tool_result","content":"5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n152:## Open issues\n166:## Scanners\n181:**LLM scanners (`prompt.txt`) file under extra rules.** `autoclose: true` on one is a config error: an LLM scanner never closes an issue for absence. `every` defaults to at least 24h and a value below 24h is an error; `max_budget_usd`, when set, must be above 0. An LLM fingerprint is `rule:path` or `rule:path:symbol` (rule `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}`, a clean relative path without `..` that is also listed in the finding's `files` and exists, symbol `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]{0,127}`); a bad fingerprint is reported by finding index only, never by content. Before grouping, a finding whose title is a near-duplicate of an open issue's title or checklist line, or of a more severe finding in the same run, is skipped without a ledger row, so it is offered again next run. Titles are lowercased, reduced to `[a-z0-9]` tokens, minus one-character tokens and a short stop list; they match when the token sets are equal, or when both have at least three tokens and their Jaccard similarity is at least 0.8. An issue filed by a scanner whose route is not `pending` ends with a footer explaining that a human adds `ard:pending` to send it to triage or closes it as not planned to dismiss it. The stale closer also considers an open issue whose only `ard:` label is its scanner label (route `none`). The engine that runs `prompt.txt` scanners is not wired in yet.\n240:## Triage loop\n281:## Executors and the Reaper\n289:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n300:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n321:## Worker loop\n327:3. **Steps.** One worker job is one state row, one pool slot and one attempt, but it runs several executor jobs, each in a fresh worktree detached at a SHA: the agent step (`<job id>`) at the branch head, or `origin/main` for a new branch; a test step (`<job id>t<k>`) and a fix step (`<job id>f<k>`) at the SHA the last push made. The branch itself belongs to the committer. Each step's handle is saved before it starts, so the Reaper's heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too. `timeout` covers the whole job: each step gets the time that is left, and with under two minutes left the loop stops and reports the tests as failed (\"ran out of time\"). `max_budget_usd` likewise caps the whole job: each agent step gets the cap minus the cost of the earlier steps (a step that reports no cost is assumed free and logged), and when nothing is left the job fails with `job budget exhausted`.\n341:## Reviewer loop\n374:## Merger loop\n399:## PR watch loop\n413:## Configuration\n417:**Shared roles.** `roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads role directories from another repository, so several repositories can reuse one role set. It is pinned like a sidecar: `ref` defaults to the shared repository's default branch (its remote HEAD, so `master` works too) and may be a branch, a tag or a full commit SHA. The controller fetches the ref shallowly, merges the shared repository's `.ard/scanners`, `triage`, `workers` and `reviewers` directories into the config snapshot, and a directory of the same kind and name in this repository wins whole, never file by file. `overrides` apply to shared roles too. `ard serve` re-reads the key with the rest of the config. If the shared repository cannot be fetched, or a shared role is invalid, the config fails to load and the controller counts as paused. Trust: the key lives in `.ard/config.yaml`, a protected path, but a branch or tag in the shared repository can change the prompts agents run without a PR here, so pin a full commit SHA.\n434:max_pods: 3                  # job slots shared by every loop\n438:budget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its own; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\n439:max_budget_usd: 5            # per job; provisional, same recalibration\n470:**Models are tiered (decided, #8).** Opus 5.5 for the lead, architect and security review; Sonnet 5.5 for workers, triage engineer and most reviewers; Haiku 5.5 for lint and docs work. The `budget_usd_per_day: 50` and per-job `max_budget_usd` values are provisional and are recalibrated from measured cost per job and per merged PR between Gate 1 and Gate 2.\n472:Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n478:## Guardrails\n495:- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. The `budget` loop posts one comment on the status issue per capped scope (the repo, or a role) per UTC day, and records a `budget_capped` event; that event also keeps a restart from posting the notice again (a crash between comment and event can repeat one notice). The comment holds only numbers, the day and config names, no agent text and no `@`; it says the dollar figure is an estimate on a subscription and how to raise the cap (edit `budget_usd_per_day` in `.ard/config.yaml` through a reviewed PR). Under `--dry-run` the comment goes to the ledger. `--loops budget` selects the notice loop; the gate is part of the pool and is always on.\n496:- **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n500:## Gaps in the original sketch\n520:## Delivery\n531:## Rollout\n546:**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`. The Gate 1 soak week does not run on the Mac: it runs on spark as `ard serve --dry-run` (one pod, `Recreate`, state on a persistent volume), and the week counts from the first healthy pod; no other `ard serve` for the same repo runs meanwhile, so one ledger is counted. See Delivery. ard supports macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported.\n558:## Open questions\ndocs/proposal.md            design source of truth\ndocs/layout.md              this file: every directory and package\ndocs/TODO.md                open issues by phase, generated from the milestones\ndocs/runbooks/              operator runbooks (gate runs, live checks)\n```\nbot-commits.yml\nci.yml\nversion.yml\n.github/workflows/version.yml:5:# semver:minor, else patch. ard's own main-lane commits (committer ARD_BOT_LOGIN)\n.github/workflows/version.yml:65:          BOT_LOGIN: ${{ vars.ARD_BOT_LOGIN }}\n.github/workflows/version.yml:98:            fail \"set the ARD_BOT_LOGIN repository variable to the App's <slug>[bot] login\"\ndocs/proposal.md:196:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\ndocs/proposal.md:548:Phase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is exercised. The ledger is `effects.jsonl` in that directory: every intended effect (issue, label, comment, commit, push, merge) is appended as one JSON line, and Gate 1 counts duplicate issues there by repeated `<!-- ard:fp=... -->` markers in `CreateIssue` bodies. Gate 1 needs only the deterministic path (CLI, config, GitHub client, effects, state, labels, executor, committer, controller, pool and the cve and stale scanners); the agent adapter, close-out and redaction are built during the soak week.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ce73ad0-b6b7-42dc-b456-d663d5942b35","uuid":"6559e8b5-204a-4bda-ac67-c490bf0ab661","timestamp":"2026-11-24T13:55:17.794Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n152:## Open issues\n166:## Scanners\n181:**LLM scanners (`prompt.txt`) file under extra rules.** `autoclose: true` on one is a config error: an LLM scanner never closes an issue for absence. `every` defaults to at least 24h and a value below 24h is an error; `max_budget_usd`, when set, must be above 0. An LLM fingerprint is `rule:path` or `rule:path:symbol` (rule `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}`, a clean relative path without `..` that is also listed in the finding's `files` and exists, symbol `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]{0,127}`); a bad fingerprint is reported by finding index only, never by content. Before grouping, a finding whose title is a near-duplicate of an open issue's title or checklist line, or of a more severe finding in the same run, is skipped without a ledger row, so it is offered again next run. Titles are lowercased, reduced to `[a-z0-9]` tokens, minus one-character tokens and a short stop list; they match when the token sets are equal, or when both have at least three tokens and their Jaccard similarity is at least 0.8. An issue filed by a scanner whose route is not `pending` ends with a footer explaining that a human adds `ard:pending` to send it to triage or closes it as not planned to dismiss it. The stale closer also considers an open issue whose only `ard:` label is its scanner label (route `none`). The engine that runs `prompt.txt` scanners is not wired in yet.\n240:## Triage loop\n281:## Executors and the Reaper\n289:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n300:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n321:## Worker loop\n327:3. **Steps.** One worker job is one state row, one pool slot and one attempt, but it runs several executor jobs, each in a fresh worktree detached at a SHA: the agent step (`<job id>`) at the branch head, or `origin/main` for a new branch; a test step (`<job id>t<k>`) and a fix step (`<job id>f<k>`) at the SHA the last push made. The branch itself belongs to the committer. Each step's handle is saved before it starts, so the Reaper's heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too. `timeout` covers the whole job: each step gets the time that is left, and with under two minutes left the loop stops and reports the tests as failed (\"ran out of time\"). `max_budget_usd` likewise caps the whole job: each agent step gets the cap minus the cost of the earlier steps (a step that reports no cost is assumed free and logged), and when nothing is left the job fails with `job budget exhausted`.\n341:## Reviewer loop\n374:## Merger loop\n399:## PR watch loop\n413:## Configuration\n417:**Shared roles.** `roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads role directories from another repository, so several repositories can reuse one role set. It is pinned like a sidecar: `ref` defaults to the shared repository's default branch (its remote HEAD, so `master` works too) and may be a branch, a tag or a full commit SHA. The controller fetches the ref shallowly, merges the shared repository's `.ard/scanners`, `triage`, `workers` and `reviewers` directories into the config snapshot, and a directory of the same kind and name in this repository wins whole, never file by file. `overrides` apply to shared roles too. `ard serve` re-reads the key with the rest of the config. If the shared repository cannot be fetched, or a shared role is invalid, the config fails to load and the controller counts as paused. Trust: the key lives in `.ard/config.yaml`, a protected path, but a branch or tag in the shared repository can change the prompts agents run without a PR here, so pin a full commit SHA.\n434:max_pods: 3                  # job slots shared by every loop\n438:budget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its own; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\n439:max_budget_usd: 5            # per job; provisional, same recalibration\n470:**Models are tiered (decided, #8).** Opus 5.5 for the lead, architect and security review; Sonnet 5.5 for workers, triage engineer and most reviewers; Haiku 5.5 for lint and docs work. The `budget_usd_per_day: 50` and per-job `max_budget_usd` values are provisional and are recalibrated from measured cost per job and per merged PR between Gate 1 and Gate 2.\n472:Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n478:## Guardrails\n495:- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. The `budget` loop posts one comment on the status issue per capped scope (the repo, or a role) per UTC day, and records a `budget_capped` event; that event also keeps a restart from posting the notice again (a crash between comment and event can repeat one notice). The comment holds only numbers, the day and config names, no agent text and no `@`; it says the dollar figure is an estimate on a subscription and how to raise the cap (edit `budget_usd_per_day` in `.ard/config.yaml` through a reviewed PR). Under `--dry-run` the comment goes to the ledger. `--loops budget` selects the notice loop; the gate is part of the pool and is always on.\n496:- **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n500:## Gaps in the original sketch\n520:## Delivery\n531:## Rollout\n546:**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`. The Gate 1 soak week does not run on the Mac: it runs on spark as `ard serve --dry-run` (one pod, `Recreate`, state on a persistent volume), and the week counts from the first healthy pod; no other `ard serve` for the same repo runs meanwhile, so one ledger is counted. See Delivery. ard supports macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported.\n558:## Open questions\ndocs/proposal.md            design source of truth\ndocs/layout.md              this file: every directory and package\ndocs/TODO.md                open issues by phase, generated from the milestones\ndocs/runbooks/              operator runbooks (gate runs, live checks)\n```\nbot-commits.yml\nci.yml\nversion.yml\n.github/workflows/version.yml:5:# semver:minor, else patch. ard's own main-lane commits (committer ARD_BOT_LOGIN)\n.github/workflows/version.yml:65:          BOT_LOGIN: ${{ vars.ARD_BOT_LOGIN }}\n.github/workflows/version.yml:98:            fail \"set the ARD_BOT_LOGIN repository variable to the App's <slug>[bot] login\"\ndocs/proposal.md:196:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub