{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BYksF1CDKZnFfkXnbeQamh","type":"tool_result","content":"**Branch protection and the bot.** The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main` with `[skip ci]`, which neither a \"require a pull request\" rule nor a required status check can admit, so the App must be on the bypass list of `main`'s ruleset (mode Always). GitHub's bypass covers the whole ruleset, not single rules (found on Gate 2, #39). ard keeps that bypass narrow itself: the committer refuses any path outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, the merger merges only when every review passed and required checks are green on the current head (checked in code, not left to GitHub), and the bot-commit audit flags any bot commit on `main` that touches anything else. GitHub enforces nothing on the App's merges, so the merger reads the required checks itself from the branch rules API. A squash merge has GitHub (`noreply@github.com`) as committer and the App as author, so the audit, which keys on the committer, does not cover merges: the merger's gates do.\n160:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits\n224:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; \n254:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role fi\n389:- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list\n445:merge:                       # defaults written by `ard init`; the list is the only source (no floor in code)\ninternal/cli/cmd_labels.go:11:\t\tUse:   \"labels\",\ninternal/cli/cmd_labels.go:16:\t\tUse:   \"sync\",\ninternal/cli/cli_test.go:12:\t